# tokopedia-scraper — credentials and defaults
#
# Copy this file to `.env` next to the scripts and fill in what you use:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is in .gitignore. Keep it there. A secret passed on a command line
# is readable by anything that can run `ps` and lands in your shell history,
# which is why none of these has a flag you are expected to type.
#
# Precedence, highest first:
#
#     explicit CLI flag  >  exported environment variable  >  .env  >  default
#
# So a `.env` never overrides something you typed, and an already-exported
# variable (a CI secret, direnv, your shell profile) is never clobbered by a
# file you forgot to delete.
#
# Check what was picked up, without printing any secret:
#
#     python3 env_config.py
#
# Every variable below is one this code actually reads, and every variable
# the code reads is below — smoke_test.py asserts the two sets are equal in
# both directions, because a documented-but-unread variable is worse than an
# undocumented one.


# --- 2Captcha -------------------------------------------------------------
# One key, four separately-billed products: captcha solving, the Scraping
# Browser API, proxies and fingerprints. https://2captcha.com
#
# Worth knowing before you buy solving for this site. Etsy sits behind
# DataDome, and its refusal page carries a `t` field that decides whether a
# solve can help at all:
#
#   t=bv  the address or the browser is banned. 2Captcha's own docs: the
#         cookie a solve returns is NOT accepted. This scraper does not pay
#         for those, and neither should you.
#   t=fe  a real slider, and the one state worth buying.
#
# Measured 2026-09-09: five solve attempts on five fresh residential exits
# produced two ERROR_CAPTCHA_UNSOLVABLE, two pages that never reached t=fe,
# and one dead exit. The key is a FALLBACK here — the Scraping Browser API
# below is the path that actually works, and it needed no solve at all.
# The key is also what --fingerprint uses.
TWOCAPTCHA_KEY=your_2captcha_api_key_here

# --- Scraping Browser API -------------------------------------------------
# A remote browser you do not run or patch, with a persistent profile and a
# chosen exit country. Used by --cdp-endpoint.
#
#   country-  picks the exit country, and therefore which STOREFRONT Etsy
#             redirects a visit to, along with its currency. Note this was
#             measured NOT being honoured on one zone (country-us returned
#             German exits on three fresh profiles), so check what you got:
#             the row `url` names the storefront that answered.
#   pid-      is a profile with persistent cookies. ONE live connection each,
#             capped per account — reuse pids rather than minting one per
#             run, and expect a 500 (`profile_locked`, exit 5) while another
#             run still holds this one.
#
#             A profile WARMS UP: one was measured refusing the first two
#             requests of a session and serving the third and everything
#             after it in full. So a block on the first fetch is a reason to
#             retry the same pid, which the engines do. A pid that keeps
#             refusing after three tries is burnt — switch to another.
#
# Note that Selenium cannot use this: chromedriver's `debuggerAddress` takes
# a bare host:port with nowhere to put a password. Playwright and pyppeteer
# authenticate on the WebSocket upgrade and work.
TOKOPEDIA_CDP_ENDPOINT=ws://{login}-zone-scraping_browser-country-us-pid-{profileId}:{password}@cb.2captcha.com:9222

# --- Proxy ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Necessary but NOT sufficient on this site, and that is the unusual part.
# Measured 2026-09-09: every datacentre address tested received HTTP 403,
# including one driving a genuine Chrome — and a headless Chromium through a
# RESIDENTIAL US exit was refused too, while plain `curl` from that same exit
# got only an interstitial. DataDome scores the network first and the browser
# second, and an automated browser fails the second test. Use
# --cdp-endpoint above unless you have a browser Etsy accepts.
#
# One more practical note: the 2Captcha residential gateway answered SOCKS5
# only on the ports tested (na.proxy.2captcha.com:2333 and :2334), and
# Chromium cannot authenticate a SOCKS5 proxy. This variable needs the HTTP
# endpoint. `na.` serves North American exits, `eu.` European ones.
#
# One URL. For a pool, use --proxy-file with one per line: a pool is what
# spreads a run's volume, and --concurrency without one just sends N times
# the traffic from a single address.
#
# The credentials here never reach the browser's command line — they go
# through the driver's own fields. Logs keep the host and port and mask the
# rest, because which exit a run used is the point of the log and is not the
# secret.
TOKOPEDIA_PROXY=http://{user}:{password}@na.proxy.2captcha.com:2334

# --- Default target -------------------------------------------------------
# Saves retyping --url. Etsy is ONE host and the locale is a path prefix
# (/de/, /uk/, /ca-fr/ — 27 of them), so there is no --country flag that
# could disagree with the URL. Your exit IP can still override the prefix: a
# request for /fr/... from a German exit came back as /de/...
#
# Any of the four listing kinds works: /search?q=..., /c/<taxonomy>,
# /market/<term>, or /shop/<name> with --mode shop. A taxonomy HUB with no
# listings on it answers 200 and yields zero rows, which is exit 4 and not a
# fault.
TOKOPEDIA_URL=https://www.etsy.com/search?q=handmade+mug

# Deliberately NOT here: an output prefix. `--out` already carries a
# non-empty default, so the loader — which only fills UNSET values — would
# never see it, and the variable would be silently inert: a setting that
# looks configurable and is not.
